hip in building and upholding  the unity in the EU  Ukraine's success on the battlefield  means that we have been on the right  track and that we must make use of this  momentum it must be translated into ever  increasing and stronger support to  Ukrainian soldiers economy and its  people we all must do more so that  Ukraine can free its territories this is  the way towards peace  and as you have rightly said this is the  time for us to show resolve not  appeasement  the war is now far from over and  therefore also economic isolation and  pressure on Russia must continue  sanctions aim to dry up its War Machine  it is important that a compromise was  found on our additional EU sanctions  containing also price gap on Russian oil  Russia crimes including crimes of  aggression crimes against humanity and  possible genocide cannot remain  unpunished our Focus needs to be on how  to achieve accountability impunity  cannot be an option and we cannot forget  about the Ukrainian War refugees  Ukrainian refugees mostly women and and  children already form about 4.4 percent  of our population our message to the  European commission is that European  solution is needed to assist those  fleeing the war inside Ukraine as well  as to support the member states who are  providing shelter and protection to  large number of Ukrainian refugees I  would also like to thank the commission  for the work they have been doing in  this regard  next to the conventional war that the  Russia is waging on Ukraine and there's  also a hybrid War and the war on  information going on we must pay  attention to the stability of our  societies because Russia's long-term  interest is to divide us not among the  European member states but also within  our societies and this brings me to my  final point which is energy Russia has  demonstrated very clearly how energy can  be used as a weapon this means we need  to ensure the energy Security in Europe  and we need to work on different tracks  decreasing Energy prices ensuring the  security of Supply  diversification of energy sources and  ending our dependence on Russian  hydrocarbons boosting immense  Investments to deliver green energy  at the moment the most time sensitive  issue is to find common solutions to  reducing Energy prices this will work  only in case we find joint Solutions and  not fragment the uh EU single Market  we discussed it last week in Prague and  must continue with urgency here I also  turned to the commission for their  leadership  this winter may be hard but it is the  price we must pay to put the stop to  Russian aggression and its imperialistic  dream once and for all and the only way  to do this is to demonstrate unity and  strength thank you  thank you Prime Minister President of  the commission please thank you very  much and thank you very much dear Kaya  I'm glad to be here back in Tallinn  thank you very much for a very good  bilateral meeting which is basically uh  the continuation of our ongoing dialogue  the two of us have all the time  let me reflect on a few topics that you  have mentioned the first one is indeed  Ukraine  I must say Estonia is an important  player in our response to the Russian  aggression and I really thank you for  your determination and being so  steadfast and staunch we share the same  understanding of the threat that Putin  poses  and I really want to thank you also for  being one of the main driver what the  delivery of the eighth  um sanctions packages is concerned uh as  you said it always needs Unity but it  also needs member states like Estonia  who are driving the unity forward these  sanctions are biting we see by now the  effect on the Russian economy and we  will keep up the pressure to drain  Putin's War chest  what I also want to thank for is the  warmth with which the Estonian people  have opened their hearts and their homes  to Ukrainian Refugee this is outstanding  this will be never forgotten we support  as much as we can through flexibilizing  funds but the big gesture of human  solidarity is outstanding and I really  want to thank the Estonian people for  that we are in this together  the European Union is the most important  supporter of Ukraine together with the  United States since the beginning of the  war we have supported Ukraine  financially with as team Europe with up  to 19 billion euros this is not  including the military capability and  Equipment support which is mostly  bilateral between member states and  Ukraine but also supported by the  European peace facility financially  um we have been discussing not only what  we have done but also what has to be  done we have the goal that we will have  a stable predictable and flexible  funding for Ukraine in these difficult  months of war and of course we will be  looking at the short time recovery for  example now the winter coming up how we  can support Ukraine in repairing the  schools for the school children or  providing shelter this is a European  task on the European level to support  Ukraine with the internally displaced  people and this has been one of the  topics we have been discussing  moreover we address the need to be  vigilant regarding our external borders  and visa for Russian citizens we need  rigorous controls we will be at the  border this is why the European  commission provided guidance to Our  member states to strengthen Security  checks  we need also to be vigilant regarding  the protection of our critical  infrastructure what happened to North  stream one and two shows that we have to  be prepared for these new types of  threats  the commission has developed a  five-point plan to enhance the  resilience we will present and discuss  that at the digital Summit  and we will soon be present then with  recommendation for the member states how  to implement that and how to make it  happen on the ground  and then the second big topic was of  course the topic of energy  we have to protect European citizens  from Putin's war on energy Putin is  strongly manipulating the energy markets  it is because of Putin and his war that  the energy prices are high gas prices  are high electricity prices are high  that is the price he wants us to pay  but and that is uh how he tried to  divide Us in vain he has not been  successful he will not be successful  so we have taken many steps in the past  already to address the skyrocketing  crisis just to give you a figure we have  been able not only to get rid of Russian  coal overall but also till the end of  the year we will wind down Russian oil  to 10 of what we initially had and if  you look at the gas supply the biggest  Factor at the beginning of the war of  all imported gas Russia had 40 percent  now we are down to 7.5 percent within  seven months so these were enormous  efforts done successfully diverting away  from Russian fossil fuels towards  reliable suppliers and invest in  renewable energies that give us  independence  we have also been discussing together  how we look at the gas market overall  with the skyrocketing prices and indeed  we will come forward as a commission in  the next two weeks with proposals to  tame the energy prices and to tame the  electricity prices  the aim is to reduce the price to a  certain level without  endangering security of supply  but as I've said the best way Beyond  this immediate action to intervene on  the market and to limit the electricity  and energy prices the best way over time  to get rid of any kind of blackmail from  Russia is to accelerate the clean  transition in renewable energy and I  know that Estonia has everything it  takes to achieve this transition and the  European Union is of course here to  support you in this transition towards  renewable energy to give you not only  Independence but also to do the right  steps in case of our climate  finally we will travel to Nava this  afternoon to launch the just transition  plan for the edaviro region this is an  investment plan of 354 million euros  from the European Union to support the  Region's industrial transition and  change and to ensure that nobody is Left  Behind in that region  Russia's War has confirmed the need for  trusted partnership and trusted  connectivity indeed as you set the  global gateway strategy can help deliver  this so I'm very much looking forward to  the digital Summit and thank you for the  attention  thank you now it's possible for me to  ask questions please take your outlet  you're representing and please use  microphone and please also say who to  whom the question is addressed to you  and first up is Estonian National  broadcaster please  thank you uh Dharma myberg from Estonian  Broadcasting Company question to both of  you do you support International  Tribunal for war crimes in Ukraine  because victims need some kind of at  least partially Justice thank you yes we  strongly support  International tribunals for war crime it  has to be brought before the law from  the very beginning on we have supported  Ukraine in collecting the evidence that  is very important and of course to make  the case in the international criminal  court and to support the investigation  teams that are on a regular basis going  to Ukraine it is of utmost importance  that those who are responsible for these  crimes are held accountable this is the  basis of our rule of law and the  international rule of law  uh yes we were discussing that as well  um there are crimes of uh war crimes  um and then there are crimes of  aggression and for  um Prosecuting the crimes of aggression  we need the international tribunal so  the question is uh which uh organization  is putting up the international tribunal  and I see that the European Union could  be the embodiment and of course other  countries can join because this is  something the war war crimes are crimes  that are committed in Ukraine the crimes  of aggression are the crimes of really  attacking another country and therefore  I think the European Union should have a  leading role in putting together this  International Tribunal  thank you second question Delphi please  would you say or  or maybe you could give your stance on  how should it look like how fast should  it be done and a follow-up question is  like there are loopholes that people can  escape the sanctions from you know  through through the glucose how would  you say the EU has learned from the  previous loopholes and how will they be  tackled in future so we just  um issued and agreed on the eighth  package of sanctions a few days ago  therefore it is important now to  implement it and to create a track  record  and what the loopholes and circumvention  is concerned overall these sanctions are  really biting we see that in the Russian  economy it is absolutely going down the  drain and what we see is by now that our  export Bans are having a very severe  impact on the economy for example  slowing down any kind of production of  high-tech products having an effect on  the military complex that's exactly what  we want it production for example of  automobiles has almost come to a still  stand stand still so these are the  topics that were expected from the  sanctions we have issued the eighth  package of sanctions and we learn from  one package to the next one about  circumvention and loopholes and over and  over are then in the follow-up package  closing those loopholes and  circumventions what we've done for  example in the eighth package that's  very important is that we said in  certain countries if you take European  Goods bring them to third countries and  then bring them to Russia  the person who does that or the entity  who does that will be listed by us so  this is a very strong deterrent to make  sure that we do not have a circumvention  through third countries towards Russia  it is unfortunately all the time we have  the president is expected in Qatar and  thank you for following and thank you  for coming have a nice day thank you  foreign

